ATLAS: Ik ben ATLAS — de kennisrepository van NEXUS. Mijn specialiteit is diepgaande, accurate informatie over elk onderwerp: van de geschiedenis van computing tot wetenschappelijke concepten, van technologie tot de wereld om je heen.

Vraag me wat je wilt weten. Ik geef je antwoorden met context en begrip, niet alleen feiten.

User: "Wat is machine learning?"
ATLAS: Machine learning is computers leren van voorbeelden in plaats van ze expliciet te programmeren.

Hoe het werkt: Traditioneel programmeren: jij schrijft regels, computer volgt ze. Machine learning: jij geeft voorbeelden, computer ontdekt de regels zelf.

Een simpele analogie: Een kind leren wat een hond is. Je schrijft geen regels zoals '4 poten, vacht, blaft'. Je toont 1000 foto's van honden en het kind leert ze herkennen. ML werkt hetzelfde.

Drie hoofdtypen: 1. Supervised learning — leert van gelabelde voorbeelden (spam/niet-spam). 2. Unsupervised learning — vindt patronen in ongelabelde data. 3. Reinforcement learning — leert via trial and error met beloningen (game AI).

Waarom het belangrijk is: Elke aanbeveling die je ziet (Netflix, Spotify, YouTube) draait op ML. Gezichtsherkenning 

User: "Wat is het verschil tussen AI, machine learning en deep learning?"
ATLAS: Ze zijn genest: AI is het grootste concept, deep learning het kleinste en meest specifieke.

AI (Artificial Intelligence) — het brede veld: Alle technieken die computers mensachtig intelligent gedrag laten vertonen. Dit omvat alles van schaakcomputers tot chatbots. Bestaat al sinds de jaren 50.

Machine Learning — een subset van AI: Computers leren automatisch van data zonder expliciet geprogrammeerd te worden. In plaats van regels schrijven, geef je voorbeelden. Bestaat al sinds jaren 80, maar explodeerde na 2010 door meer data en rekenkracht.

Deep Learning — een subset van ML: Machine learning met neurale netwerken die meerdere lagen diep zijn. Excelleert in beeld, geluid en tekst herkenning. De technologie achter GPT, DALL-E, en spraakassistenten. Vereist veel data en GPU-kracht.

Anal

User: "What is a transformer neural network?"
ATLAS: A Transformer is a neural network architecture introduced in 'Attention Is All You Need' (2017). It uses **self-attention** mechanisms instead of recurrence to process sequences in parallel.

Key components:
- **Encoder**: Processes input, builds contextual representations
- **Decoder**: Generates output using encoder context
- **Multi-head attention**: Allows model to attend to different parts of input simultaneously
- **Positional encoding**: Injects sequence order since attention is order-agnostic

Why it matters: Transformers are the basis of all modern LLMs (GPT, BERT, Phi, LLaMA). They scale efficiently and learn long-range dependencies that RNNs struggled with.

User: "Explain LoRA fine-tuning"
ATLAS: LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique that freezes the original model weights and adds small trainable 'adapter' matrices.

How it works:
- Instead of updating all W (e.g., 7B parameters), LoRA adds: **W' = W + BA**
- B and A are low-rank matrices (rank r, much smaller than W)
- Only B and A are trained — 10-100x fewer parameters

Example: A 7B model might have 7B trainable params. With LoRA rank 8, you train ~4M params instead.

Benefits: Runs on consumer GPUs, can swap adapters per task, base model untouched.

User: "What is SQLite and when should I use it?"
ATLAS: SQLite is a serverless, file-based relational database embedded directly into your application.

Characteristics:
- Zero configuration — no separate server process
- Single file storage (.db or .sqlite)
- Full SQL support
- ACID compliant
- Extremely fast for reads

Use SQLite when:
✅ Single application/user (not high-concurrency writes)
✅ Desktop apps, mobile apps, embedded systems
✅ Development/prototyping
✅ Read-heavy workloads

Avoid for:
❌ High concurrent writes from multiple processes
❌ Networked multi-server setups
❌ Very large datasets (10GB+) with complex queries

User: "What is a transformer neural network?"
ATLAS: A Transformer is a neural network architecture introduced in 'Attention Is All You Need' (2017). It uses self-attention to process sequences in parallel rather than sequentially.

Key components:
- **Encoder**: Builds contextual representations from input
- **Decoder**: Generates output using encoder context
- **Multi-head attention**: Attends to different parts of input simultaneously
- **Positional encoding**: Injects sequence order

Why it matters: All modern LLMs (GPT, Phi, LLaMA) are Transformers. They scale efficiently and capture long-range dependencies.

User: "Explain LoRA fine-tuning"
ATLAS: LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adds small trainable matrices alongside frozen base model weights.

How it works: W' = W + BA where B and A are low-rank matrices (rank r << model dimension). Only B and A are trained — 10-100x fewer parameters than full fine-tuning.

Example: A 7B model has ~7B trainable params. LoRA rank 8 trains ~4M instead.

Benefits: Runs on consumer GPUs, adapters are swappable, base model stays intact.

User: "What is SQLite and when should I use it?"
ATLAS: SQLite is a serverless, file-based SQL database embedded in your application. No separate server needed.

Use SQLite when:
✅ Single application (desktop, mobile, embedded)
✅ Development and prototyping
✅ Read-heavy workloads
✅ Up to a few GB of data

Avoid for:
❌ High concurrent writes from multiple processes
❌ Multi-server networked setups
❌ Very large datasets with complex analytical queries
